# Instructions

Does the target text at checks.{{ name }} BEGIN a fresh spoken turn, with its own speaker attribution before or within its utterance, rather than finish the preceding utterance or its reporting clause?

Use the full three-page local_raw_evidence and preceding_source_index to identify the genuine preceding MAIN-TEXT utterance. Ownership and immediate_paragraph_hint are fallible locators, not a fixed edge. Exclude ancillary material and do not invent participants or text beyond the supplied pages.

Ordinary quotation marks can enclose terms, titles or cited passages rather than dialogue. Do not certify a spoken turn from quotation marks, a new sentence, a conjunction or topical coherence alone.

A narrative introduction saying a participant answers, replies or speaks, followed by that participant's new utterance, can begin a fresh turn. A reporting clause placed AFTER a preceding quote can instead belong to that preceding utterance and does not establish a new turn.

This question asks whether a fresh turn starts at this boundary, not whether its speaker differs from the previous speaker. Do not require a split merely for narration after a quote. If no actual new attributed turn is established, answer FALSE.

# True

This target begins a new attributed spoken turn, rather than completing the previous utterance/reporting clause.

# False

A fresh attributed turn at this boundary is not established.
